 :findings [{:id :scope-policy-not-yet-defined
             :severity :blocking
             :evidence "parse-function recursively parses the range using opts; parse-object-path accepts any NameRef symbol, while runtime aliases inherit into nested scopes. The plan simultaneously requires unknown-name errors, unsupported outer-capture errors and preservation of independent nested legacy symbols, but does not define the marker, inheritance, or resolution order that distinguishes them."
             :risk "Passing an outer alias into a nested result can accidentally resolve it to that nested arity's same integer index; clearing all scope can silently preserve an attempted capture as a free legacy symbol. Blanket strictness breaks legacy symbols."
             :required "Specify scope frames and exact behavior for nested named and unnamed arities, shadowing, sibling alternatives, and free legacy symbols before implementation. If existing syntax cannot disambiguate the competing cases without a compatibility change, narrow the admitted grammar or obtain a policy decision; never silently lower an outer reference."
             :oracles ["RED positive: nested same-spelling inner binder resolves only to inner index; baseline cannot parse the binder." "Negative contract: nested reference to an outer binder with no inner declaration must explicitly fail under the chosen admitted context; compare an independent nested legacy symbol that must remain unchanged. Check nested return types inside filter types as well as range types." "Compare independently scoped IFn alternatives and verify an unknown subject cannot inherit a sibling binder. Negative cases may already fail on baseline; GREEN must fail for the intended reason."]}
            {:id :fixed-prefix-classification-must-follow-parse-cat
             :severity :blocking
             :evidence "parse-cat accepts both postfix repetition/dotted operators and parsed regex type forms, and distribute-regex-arities recursively expands :cat, :alt, :? and other regexes. A single source form is not necessarily one fixed argument. parse-ast identifies raw-dom by token positions for *, :.., &, <* and <..., then groups triples; it is not the checker regex grammar."
             :risk "Counting triples or superficially counting forms can bind a later name to the wrong index; a name on a repeated form can be stripped and mistakenly accepted as a fixed argument. A syntactically plain named type can itself parse to a regex."
             :required "Define a conservative admissibility algorithm based on parsed domain components and operator association: require each named slot and all preceding slots to prove exactly one mandatory argument, reject a named component associated with any repetition/dotted/keyword operator, and reject after an unproved variable-width component. Preserve original unnamed forms and their warnings. Reconcile the admitted subset with runtime raw-dom rather than assuming grammar parity."
             :oracles ["RED positive: a named slot after an unnamed fixed slot and a named mandatory prefix before a supported rest compare exactly with their legacy positional equivalents." "Negative contract: reject name on rest/dotted/optional, name after optional or alternation, and named regex-valued component; include a preceding nested cat/alt form whose expansion changes width. Also verify unnamed regex/rest/dotted inputs retain their previous representation and warnings." "Exercise [a :- T * :-> R] against raw-dom reasoning: runtime removes T from fixed-dom before grouping, so it is not evidence that named rest is supported."]}
            {:id :positional-lowering-and-test-contract-incomplete
             :severity :blocking
             :evidence "Object IDs are passed to orep/-path after the integer warning, whereas is/! pass the subject directly to proposition constructors; logical proposition parsing recursively calls parse-proposition. Parsed Result is distributed unchanged across generated arities; funapp.cljc 261-265 opens a Result against argument objects and types. Runtime symbol-object shorthand is distinct from checker parse-object's map/empty/no-object cases."
             :risk "Reusing integer source parsing emits a new warning; applying only object resolution misses filters; syntax-only representation equality cannot establish substitution of the right caller object, and generic rewriting can alter legacy empty/no-object or nested IDs. The runtime shorthand examples do not establish checker support."
             :required "Define a shared named-subject resolver at the object-ID and is/! subject boundaries, not an indiscriminate tree rewrite; retain legacy code path and error/warning behavior for literal integers and legacy symbols. Explicitly restrict this slice to map object syntax unless separately designed. Establish positional substitution by the real application path, including result and filter paths, not by parse success alone."
             :oracles ["RED positive: parse named object and then/else propositions, including nested and path-bearing propositions; compare IDs, polarity, paths, results and arities to literal positional equivalents. Named object emits no integer warning while literal integer object retains its existing warning; do not assume proposition integers warn, since the shown parser does not issue that warning." "Behavior: typecheck a two-argument function and applications/refinements that distinguish argument 1 from argument 0, including a negative wrong-neighbor branch, nil/empty/nonempty seq, and path-bearing cases. Preserve empty and no-object representations. No execution is authorized by this review."]}
            {:id :red-oracle-must-distinguish-rejection-reasons
             :severity :material
             :evidence "The previous report records that the current checker already rejects a named domain with Cannot resolve type: coll; current parse-cat parses domain tokens as types before any named-scope check. Therefore a test asserting only that malformed, duplicate, unknown or captured names throw may already pass before the implementation."
             :risk "An exception-only negative test can pass RED and GREEN even if the new scope safety check never runs; requiring every negative case to fail on baseline is not a coherent RED requirement."
             :required "Partition positive feature tests, which should fail baseline and pass candidate, from negative guard tests, which must assert a stable parser diagnostic or structured reason for the specific guard after named syntax is admitted. Run exactly the same suite at baseline and candidate, document baseline negative outcomes rather than demanding all be red, and ensure a deliberately unsafe candidate would fail each guard oracle."
             :oracles ["Baseline positive named seq and positional/filter equivalence fail due to unsupported binder syntax; candidate passes." "Candidate duplicate, unknown, cross-arity, unsupported capture and variable-domain tests must fail for their respective guard reasons, not Cannot resolve type for the binder. Existing integer warning remains independently asserted."]}]
